  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Task Force: ORCID in Books Workflows (2018) in Figshare.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Task Force: ORCID in Books Workflows (2018) in Figshare is authored by ORCID, Alice Meadows and Tony Alves. Topics: Task (project management), Workflow, Computer science, Task force and World Wide Web.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2018 ORCID task force report documents a focused community effort on books workflows, but the measurable impact record is still sparse. The article currently shows no citations, no online mentions, and no scite appraisal activity, so the evidence base is quiet rather than expansive.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Task Force: ORCID in Books Workflows is a scholarly article published in Figshare in 2018 by ORCID and a large multi-author team. The abstract identifies it as a description of the ORCID in Books Workflows Task Force, which ran from August 2016 to September 2017. Concept tagging places the work at the intersection of workflow design, task-force coordination, database and web infrastructure, and broader systems-oriented questions relevant to scholarly publishing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record suggests a practice- and implementation-focused contribution rather than a conventional empirical article. Its subject matter is specific: it documents a community process around integrating ORCID into books-related workflows, an area where operational detail and stakeholder coordination are often central. That framing helps explain why the article is most clearly legible as a record of work done, rather than as a paper with a large citation footprint.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Available impact indicators are limited. OpenAlex currently records zero citations, and scite shows no citing, supporting, or contradicting mentions. Event Data also reports no online mentions, and the DataCite record shows zero views and zero downloads. Taken together, these signals indicate that the work has not, at least in the sources collected here, generated measurable scholarly or public attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are, however, signs of formal dissemination and indexing. The item is openly available, is recognized as a journal article in the metadata, and appears in OpenAIRE in multiple repository instances. The presence of related identifier metadata, including a linked earlier ORCID-in-books resource, also shows that the report sits within a broader documented workflow initiative. In short, the record supports its role as a traceable project output, even though the current evidence does not show downstream uptake.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
